{"id": "ind_gc_e7b4d1bdf991", "answer_text": "PIK3R1 encodes the p85α regulatory subunit of class IA PI3Ks that stabilizes and restrains p110 catalytic subunits; upon receptor activation p110 produces PIP3, recruiting PDK1/AKT and activating mTOR to control growth, metabolism and immune functions. Germline PIK3R1 variants have divergent, mutation-class–dependent effects: some impair p85α function and reduce PI3K→AKT signaling causing SHORT syndrome phenotypes (short stature, partial lipodystrophy, insulin resistance), while specific heterozygous splice or dominant variants disrupt inhibitory control over p110δ and cause constitutive PI3Kδ activity in lymphocytes (APDS2) with immune dysregulation. Somatic PIK3R1 mutations occur recurrently in cancers and dysregulate PI3K→AKT→mTOR in a mutation- and tumor-context–dependent manner. Therapeutic approaches target catalytic p110 isoforms (isoform-selective PI3K inhibitors, e.g., p110δ inhibitors for APDS2; p110α inhibitors in some cancers) or downstream mTOR, but drug choice and predicted response require variant-, isoform- and cell-type–specific evidence. Recommended next steps to fully validate and graph-anchor this mechanism: run targeted KG/database queries (HGNC/UniProt; OMIM/ClinVar/ClinGen/GenCC; COSMIC/TCGA/cBioPortal; Reactome/KEGG/GO), retrieve variant-level functional literature (phospho-AKT/p-S6 data), and perform functional assays (phospho-flow/western for AKT/S6, immune-cell signaling and insulin‑stimulated AKT assays) before making per-variant therapeutic assertions.", "predicted_genes": [], "predicted_pathways": [], "predicted_phenotypes": [], "predicted_drugs": [], "predicted_relations": [], "bridge_found": false, "mechanism_completeness": null, "graph_faithfulness": null, "utility_score": null, "tool_calls": [], "notes": "experiment=llm_only; max_rounds=3; selected_roles=['GeneCurator', 'DiseaseMechanismAnalyst', 'PathwayBiologist', 'PhenotypeMapper', 'EvidenceReviewer', 'DrugMechanismAnalyst']"}
